id: charging-assault
name: Charging Assault
kind: signature
side: advantages
tpCost: 10
requirement: N/A
description: >
  Your attack lets you rush forward and turn your momentum into power.
text: >
  At Attack Declaration, you may move up to your Boosted Speed in a straight line
  towards your Opponent before any rolls, as long as your Movement would end with
  them in your Melee Range. If you move more than 3 Squares through this effect,
  increase your Wound Roll by 1 for each Square you moved after the third. This
  bonus cannot exceed 2(T).

  If you are in a High Environment, you may move up or down 1 rank of High
  Environment when using this effect. If you are in the Low Sky Environment, you
  may leave the High Environments entirely and return to a typical Battle
  Environment for the Square that you end your movement on.

---

# No script. What this does is move a character, and the language has no verb for
# that - nor should it, since where you may go is a question about the map.
#
# So the movement is the player's to make, on the map, as they would make any
# other. What the system asks for is the one number the rule turns into arithmetic:
# how many Squares were covered. That is asked when the attack is declared, which is
# when the rule says the movement happens, and it feeds two bonuses:
#
#   - this Advantage's own: +1 per Square past the third, capped at 2(T)
#   - the Blitz Profile's, when Blitz is what granted this: +1/2 your Agility
#     Modifier if the distance exceeded your Normal Speed
#
# The second belongs to Blitz and lives with Blitz. The two meet through the number
# and nowhere else, which is what keeps an Advantage from being welded to whichever
# Profile happens to hand it out - Blitz is one way in, a Signature Technique that
# buys this for 10 TP is another, and neither knows about the other.
#
# Not enforced, and deliberately: the straight line, the Melee Range the movement
# has to end in, the Boosted Speed ceiling, and the High Environment ranks. Those
# are all the map's business, and the player moving the token has already answered
# them by moving it.
